From: Lance <Gero3977@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] avcodec/v4l2_buffers: default to microseconds when no timebase is set

v4l2_get_timebase() returns pkt_timebase if set and time_base otherwise.
A caller that sets neither (both default to 0/1) gets every V4L2 buffer
timestamp written as pts * 0/1 = 0, and reading it back divides by zero
in av_rescale_q(), so every decoded frame has pts == AV_NOPTS_VALUE.

Firefox's V4L2-DRM path (FFmpegVideoDecoder::InitV4L2Decoder) sets
neither and passes microsecond timestamps. With h264_v4l2m2m on a
Qualcomm Venus decoder it gets a frame with pts=-9223372036854775808,
flushes, and falls back to software decoding.

Fall back to the V4L2 timestamp unit itself (microseconds, as
AV_TIME_BASE_Q) when
neither timebase is set.

Signed-off-by: Lance <Gero3977@gmail.com>
Assisted-by: Claude-Code:claude-opus-5-5
---
 libavcodec/v4l2_buffers.c | 4 +++-
 1 file changed, 3 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

--- a/libavcodec/v4l2_buffers.c
+++ b/libavcodec/v4l2_buffers.c
@@ -58,7 +58,9 @@ static inline AVRational v4l2_get_timebase(V4L2Buffer *avbuf)
 
     if (s->avctx->pkt_timebase.num)
         return s->avctx->pkt_timebase;
-    return s->avctx->time_base;
+    if (s->avctx->time_base.num)
+        return s->avctx->time_base;
+    return v4l2_timebase; /* microseconds */
 }
 
 static inline void v4l2_set_pts(V4L2Buffer *out, int64_t pts)
